      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-98816",
        "knowledge_point_id": "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications",
        "problem": "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a node\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=1$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?",
        "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-4,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-4$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-4$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096422/assets/9b72426d7680ded1722e2f9270c96ac210fbe11cc1febd9390061177d239646c.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096422/assets/742d50bf56c55ad235ae96cdfc47db965f1e7b2197fee858d2fbac41299b34e2.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-4,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to converge toward this line.",
