    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096470",
      "id": "q-72345",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-72345",
        "knowledge_point_id": "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004",
        "knowledge_point": "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither",
        "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the line $y=x$ and the curve $y={x}^{3},$ in the first quadrant?\n\nI. It is a type I region\n\nII. It is a type II region\n\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region",
        "worked_solution": "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096470/assets/1d1c1cbe30e6f612f824adc76d712a98194ffccc5edefc11cccdaf07204513ba.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
        "answer_fields": [
