        "problem": "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x-2)^{2}=12(y+1)?$",
        "worked_solution": "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(3)(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=3.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=2$ and $k=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(2,-1).$",
        "answer_fields": [
